course: geography
lesson: 63
title: Olympiad: maps, directions and clocks ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This olympiad lesson mixes the first lessons of the course - scale, compass points, latitude and longitude, and time zones - into puzzles where one careful step after another cracks each one.
explain: Direction puzzles are about turns: a right turn is a quarter turn clockwise, so if you face north and turn right twice you face south, and turning right four times brings you back where you began.
explain: Some puzzles hide a trap in the place: at the North Pole every way you can walk is south, so a house there could have all four walls facing south, and the bear at the window would be a white polar bear.
explain: Clock puzzles need two steps: first add the hours you travelled on your own clock, then change to the clock of the place you land in; mixing up the order is the classic mistake.
explain: Picture your mate from Leeds moaning "Tokyo's like a million hours ahead, innit": the million is just a way of saying "a lot", and in winter the real gap is 9 hours, which you can work out without panic.
fact: A right turn is a quarter turn clockwise: facing north, one right turn faces you east, two face you south and three face you west.
fact: At the North Pole every direction is south, and at the South Pole every direction is north.
fact: In Britain and other lands north of the Tropic of Cancer, the Sun at midday is due south, so your midday shadow points north; in Sydney and other lands south of the Tropic of Capricorn it is the other way round.
fact: On a 1:50,000 map 2 cm stands for 1 km, and on a 1:25,000 map 4 cm stands for 1 km, so the same walk looks twice as long on the 1:25,000 map.
fact: On a grid reference the eastings (along the bottom) come before the northings (up the side), remembered as "along the corridor, then up the stairs".
fact: One degree of latitude is about 111 km, so going 2 degrees straight north is a journey of about 222 km.
fact: The Earth turns 15 degrees of longitude every hour, so two places 45 degrees apart see local noon 3 hours apart, and the place further east sees it first.
fact: In winter the UK uses GMT, equal to UTC; then New York is 5 hours behind London, Los Angeles 8 hours behind, Beijing 8 hours ahead, Tokyo 9 hours ahead and India 5 and a half hours ahead.
fact: Japan, China and India do not change their clocks in summer, so when the UK moves to British Summer Time the gap to Tokyo shrinks from 9 hours to 8.
fact: Crossing the International Date Line westwards you jump forward one calendar day, and crossing it eastwards you go back one day.
fact: The equator, the Tropic of Cancer, the Tropic of Capricorn and the Arctic Circle are lines of latitude, while the prime meridian at Greenwich and the 180 degree line are lines of longitude.
fact: In the northern hemisphere the Pole Star stands about as many degrees above the horizon as your latitude, and at the equator it sits right on the horizon.
fact: Eratosthenes measured the Earth around 240 BC, Mercator published his world map in 1569, Harrison's H4 watch was tested at sea in 1761, and the Greenwich prime meridian was agreed in 1884.
exercise: You face north, turn right, turn right again, then turn left. Which way do you face now? | East | North | West | South
exercise: You face west and make three right turns. Which way do you face? | South | East | North-west | North
exercise: Walk 5 km north, then 3 km east, then 5 km south. Where are you? | 3 km east of the start | Back where you started | 13 km from where you started | 3 km west of the start
exercise: A house has all four walls facing south, and a bear walks past. What colour is the bear? | White, it is a polar bear at the North Pole | Brown, it is a bear in a cold forest | Black, all bears near houses are black | Any colour, the walls tell us nothing at all
exercise: At midday in London, which way does your shadow point? | North | South, towards the Sun | East, where the Sun rose | Straight down, no shadow
exercise: At midday in Sydney, which way does your shadow point? | South | North | West | It points north, as everywhere
exercise: Two villages are 6 cm apart on a 1:50,000 map. How far apart are they really? | 3 km | 60 km | 30 km | 300 metres
exercise: A path is 3 km long. How long is it on a 1:25,000 map? | 12 cm | 6 cm | 3 cm | 75 cm
exercise: Which line is the odd one out: the equator, the Tropic of Cancer, the Arctic Circle or the prime meridian? | The prime meridian, the only line of longitude | The Arctic Circle, the only line in the north | The equator, the only line crossing any land | The Tropic of Cancer, the only line in the sea
exercise: You travel from 50 degrees north to 53 degrees north, straight north. About how far is that? | About 333 km | About 111 km | About 53 km | About 3,000 km
exercise: Local noon in town A comes 2 hours before local noon in town B. What do we know? | A is 30 degrees of longitude east of B | A is 30 degrees west of B | A is 2 degrees east of B | A is 30 degrees north of B
exercise: In January it is 12:00 in London. What time is it in Tokyo? | 21:00 | 03:00 next day | 20:00, an hour less | 13:00 in Tokyo
exercise: In July it is 12:00 in London. What time is it in Tokyo? | 20:00 | 21:00 | 19:00 | 04:00
exercise: A ship crosses the date line going west at noon on Tuesday. What day is it just after? | Wednesday | Tuesday | Monday | Thursday
exercise: A sailor sees the Pole Star 50 degrees above the horizon. Where is she compared with London, at 51.5 degrees north? | A little to the south of London | A little to the north | Far to the east | Exactly level with London
exercise: Put in order, oldest first: Mercator's map, Harrison's H4 at sea, Eratosthenes measures the Earth, the Greenwich meridian agreed. | Eratosthenes, Mercator, H4, Greenwich | Mercator, Eratosthenes, H4, Greenwich | Eratosthenes, H4, Mercator, Greenwich | Eratosthenes, Mercator, Greenwich, H4
exercise: Which grid reference rule is right? | Along the corridor, then up the stairs | Up the stairs, then along the corridor | Northings first, then eastings | Any order, both numbers mean the same
test: Your mate says "I walked 1 km south, 1 km east, 1 km north and I'm back at my tent." Where could his tent be? | At the North Pole | In the middle of London | On the equator in Kenya | Anywhere, if he walks fast
test: flight leaves London 9am in January, 7 hours to New York. what time do we land, their time? | 11am | 4pm | 9pm | 2am
test: my nan in Delhi wants a call at 6pm her time. it's winter here in Manchester, what time do I ring, our kid? ("our kid" = mate) | 12:30 | 18:00 | 23:30 | 13:00
test: Lad says "Tokyo's like a million hours ahead, innit." How far ahead is Tokyo really in winter? | 9 hours | A million hours | 1 hour | 15 hours
test: my bro says Norwich and London share a clock, so the Sun's highest at the same second, no cap. Right? | No, Norwich is east, so its noon comes a few minutes sooner | Yes, the same clock time means the same Sun | No, Norwich's noon comes an hour after London's | Yes, England is too small for any difference
test: "This hill's proper steep, mate, the lines are miles apart on the map." What's wrong? | Far-apart contour lines mean a gentle slope | Contour lines tell us nothing about steepness | Nothing, far-apart lines always mean steep | Contour lines only show the roads
test: Kid in Kashgar, far west China: "Sun's still not up at 9 and I'm late for school, ugh." Why so late? | All China runs on Beijing's clock, far to the east | The Sun rises in the west in China | Kashgar is south of the equator | China puts its clocks back every morning
test: Sis: "Greenland's as big as Africa, look at the map, I'm not delulu." Who's right? | Africa is about 14 times bigger than Greenland | They really are about the same size | Greenland is a bit bigger, as the map shows | Africa is only about twice as big
test: "We crossed the date line going east on Saturday, so we had two Saturdays, sweet as." Can that be true? | Yes, going east you go back a day | No, going east you lose a whole day | No, the date line never changes the day | Yes, but only on a leap year
test: Your gran: "It's a bit far to the next village, love." The map is 1:50,000 and they're 20 cm apart. How far is "a bit"? | 10 km, so not just a bit | 20 km | 1 km | 100 km
test: Mate says the Pole Star is overhead at the equator, he saw it on a cruise, deffo. True? | No, at the equator it sits on the horizon | Yes, it is overhead at the equator | Yes, it is overhead everywhere in the world | No, it is under the ground there
exam: Determine the direction in which an observer is facing, having commenced facing south and executed, in succession, a left turn and a further left turn. | North | South | East | West
exam: A vessel departs Tokyo at 11:00 local time in January and proceeds for twelve hours to London. Ascertain the local time of arrival. | 14:00 | 23:00 | 08:00 | 02:00
exam: Identify the location at which every possible direction of departure leads southwards. | The North Pole | The South Pole | The equator at the prime meridian | Greenwich in London
exam: Two settlements lie 45 degrees of longitude apart. Determine the interval between their respective local noons. | Three hours | Forty-five minutes | Forty-five hours | One hour and a half
